Mostly correct. Two parts need tightening.

(1) "Entire +88% is emission" (70b465b5) is an inference, not a measurement. The only v15 probe is BASE against DORMANT. The inference itself does not follow: v15 having no observeEnqueue removes one contributor, but it does not show the rest is emission.

The v16 ablation-2 residual (NO-EMIT +51%, NO-BOTH+NO-EMIT +38%, slower than BASE in 12/12 paired trials) is not purely non-emission cost. The NO-EMIT mutant replaces only the notify call, so its arguments are still built on every write: the meta spread and the [positionId] array. That residual therefore mixes two things:
- argument construction, which a guard would skip;
- the mutation-capture wrapper, which a guard cannot skip: peek before, peek after, the mutation object, observer dispatch and markOwnerInvalidated.

The residual also sits below the preregistered 3×relIQR claim threshold (72%). It is established only under the paired-trial rule.

(2) "15.3.1 with the ordinary-write change removes v15's dormant penalty" is overstated and unmeasured on every version. The change pays off only by flipping the process-global hasPathObservers() to false, which requires all four of these:
- the '**' subscription made lazy;
- onFlush made lazy, because hasObservers() also counts flushCallbacks;
- no other registrant anywhere in the process (link, devtools, restoration, diagnostics, write-observation, provenance);
- tolerance of unguarded entity, marker and transactions-interceptor notify calls.

Even then, the wrapper cost installed at build time stays. A per-owner skip needs owner-keyed subscription bookkeeping that v15's PathNotifier lacks.

Additional point: in this benchmark the '**' handler and the confirmed-turn recording run once per microtask flush, after the synchronous timed loop, because writes coalesce. So the per-write cost being measured is producing emission plus the wrapper, not the L15 contract's consumer work.
